# Coldwater (Docs/Design/62-talk.md, section 3): the lake's fishers on Glasswater, where the Long River rises. Calm;
# nets, water, weed and catch. Drafts (phase 3), to be written properly with you (phase 7).

grammar coldwater
greet = Still water | Calm water to you
greet.broad = Still water, still water
farewell = Fair winds | Calm water
agree = It'll float | Fair enough
refuse = That won't float
surprise = Well, I'll be netted | Calm, now
annoyed = Weed and tangle | Holed again
very = right
good = fair catch | fair
bad = muddied | rough
worthless = bait | net-weed
luck = a full net
child = minnow
pet!! = little fish | my pearl
rain = lake-rain | wet
tired = waterlogged
nonsense = weed
tag = , eh?
oath! = Deep and dark | Net and line | Glass and gods
home = my own shore
# (Phase 7, the rest of plain's words.)
address = neighbour | shore-friend
address[other.stage=young] = minnow | little fish
bed = my hammock | the bunk
cold = lake-cold | chill
dear! = my pearl | my catch | love
drunk = half-sunk | afloat
hot = sticky | close
hungry = empty as a holed net | hungry
hurt = sore | knocked about
little = a drop | a ripple
money = scale-coin | coin
robbery = Netted, plain as day | That's poaching, that is
saying! = Water finds its level | Still water, deep water | The lake takes what it takes
thanks = Thank you kindly | Thanks, friend
work = the nets | the catch | the work
# (Phase 7, the conversations' words.)
wages = scale-coin | pay
boss = the skipper | the net-master
busy = run off my paws | hauling all day
slow = becalmed | dead water
takings = the catch | the day's catch
cheated = netted | gutted
mum! = Mam | Ma
dad! = Da | Pa
gran = Granny | Gran
grandad = Grandad | Old Da
supper = supper | the pot
toffs = the dry-pawed lot | the quality
skint = cleaned out | holed
busybody = net-mender | gossip
fool = you great herring | you wet lump
daft = water-headed | daft
toast = Calm water | Full nets | Fair winds
drink = a jar | a dram
fog = lake-mist | the grey
wind = a blow off the lake | wind
watch = the Watch
thief = net-robber | thief
grave = the deep | the ground
grub = fish | grub
poorly = waterlogged | poorly
chill = the lake-cold | a chill
remedy = fish oil | a hot stone | willow bark
olddays = the old water | days gone by
blessing = Calm water to you | Fair winds | The lake keep you
bless = Calm water | The lake keep you
far = across the water | a long row
stranger = shore-stranger | outsider
journey = voyage | long row
# The dialogue window (doc 83).
shop_offer = What'll you have off the boats? | Have a look, shore-friend. | Fresh as the tide.
shop_dear = The sea sets the prices. | Dear as a calm day. | Rough water, dear goods.
shop_sold = There, safe aboard. | Stowed for you. | Off it goes.
shop_bye = Fair winds. | Calm water to you. | Come back with the tide.
shop_bare = The tide's taken the lot. | Shelves as bare as low water. | Nothing landed today.
talk_open = What brings you up from the water? | What is it, neighbour? | Out with it.
# A giver's work (doc 83, Phase 2).
work_offer = There's work on the water, if you'll row it. | I could use a steady oar. | Something needs carrying.
work_taken = Good. Mind the tide. | Good. Don't let it drift. | Settled, then. Off you row.
work_paid = Here, as promised, shore-friend. | Paid in full, calm water. | Fairly landed. There you are.
work_back = No harm done. Another boat will take it. | Fair enough. The tide turns. | Better put back than lost at sea.
work_none = Nothing landed for you today. | The water's quiet. No work. | Nothing needs rowing just now.
# The other roles (doc 83, Phase 3).
inn_offer = Come in off the water. Bed, board or a drink? | Dry off by the fire, shore-friend. | A bunk or a bite?
inn_bed = Upstairs, snug as a moored boat. There's a chest for your things. | Your bunk's made upstairs. Calm sleep.
watch_greet = The watch. Keep it calm water and we'll get on. | What is it, neighbour? The watch is listening.
church_greet = Calm water be on you. | Come in from the shore. All are welcome.
learn_offer = It's a trade that keeps a boat afloat. | Steady as the tide, this work.
# The dialogue trees' other words (doc 83, Phase 4): news and gossip there isn't, a trouble that isn't, thanks for
# help, the way, a resident's work, work turned down, an inn's fire and its helpers, the watch's word, a master's
# new apprentice, something to hand over; a keeper asleep.
shop_asleep = Mm... boats are in... come back with the tide. | Zzz... shut till morning, shore-friend.
news_none = Calm water, and no news. | Nothing's washed up lately. | Not a ripple.
gossip_none = Net-menders' talk. Not for me. | Nothing I'd repeat.
trouble_none = Nothing the tide won't fix. | Nothing, thank you kindly.
trouble_thanks = Calm water to you. I won't forget it. | Thank you kindly. That's a full net.
way_ask = Where are you rowing for? | Where to, shore-friend?
job_busy = This and that. Mostly the water. | I keep afloat.
work_declined = Suit yourself. | Another tide, then.
inn_rest = Dry off by the fire. A bunk upstairs rests you properly, mind. | Take the bench. Nobody'll move you.
watch_noted = Noted. We'll haul them in. | Good. The watch thanks you.
watch_bye = Calm water, and keep the peace. | Move along, then.
learn_taken = Good. Be here when the boats go out, and stay near me. | Then you're crew. Stay close while I work.
receiver_ask = Something washed up for me? | For me, shore-friend?
inn_helper = I'm only crew here. Ask #here.innkeeper# for a bunk. | #here.innkeeper# keeps the house. I carry the cups.

# Discourse words (doc 84 §10.3, Phase 7): Coldwater's hedge, account, preclose, excuse and the rest (plain.grammar
# says what each is). Drafts, to be tuned by ear.
hedge = Steady as she floats | Steady, shore-friend | Slow on the oars, now
account = I've my own boat to bail | The lake's kept me busy enough
ack = Well, well | So it floats
pivot = Speaking of which, now. | That brings another fish up.
anyway = Anyway, let that drift. | Enough of that water.
later = next calm | by and by
resume = Back to my line, then | As I was casting
preclose = The lake waits for nobody | Still. Can't stand here mooring
excuse = I'm wanted at the shore | The lake waits for nobody
knew = That's washed up already | Heard it on the shore
sympathy = A heavy net, that | Rough water, that
concede = I'll give you that one | That floats, I'll grant
insist = I'll not drift on it | Mark me, shore-friend
warn = Mind the deep water | Have a care on the jetty
welcome_back = Washed up again | Back again, shore-friend
welcome = Come aboard | Room on the bench, shore-friend
sorry = I meant no ripple | No offence, shore-friend
forgive = Water under the boat | No harm, shore-friend
deflect = Any calm day | No need, shore-friend
no_matter = Another tide, then | No matter, shore-friend
go_ahead = Cast away | Out with it, shore-friend
put_off = Not this tide | When the water's calmer
vent = Between us and the lake | Truth be told, shore-friend
reassure = It'll float, you'll see | Calm, now; it'll pass
congrats = A full net for you | Well landed
demur = That won't float with me | I'd not cast that way
doubt = Sounds fishy to me | I'd want it netted first
listen = Listen here, shore-friend | Hark

# Backchannel lines (doc 84 §10.2, Phase 7): plain's short answers ("Good.", "Always.", "It is.") as Coldwater says them.
# Drafts, to be tuned by ear; one left out falls back to plain's words.
bc_good = Fair catch. | That'll float.
bc_always = Same as the lake. | Every season, same as the lake.
bc_thank_you = Thank you kindly. | Thanks, shore-friend.
bc_it_is = That it is. | It is, deep and true.
bc_ha = Ha! Netted. | Heh.
bc_i_will = I will, steady as an anchor. | I will, calm as you like.
bc_very = Right deep. | Deep as the lake.
bc_never = Not in a month of calms. | Never, not once.
bc_probably = Like as not. | Likely, as the lake goes.
bc_we_will_see = We'll see what the lake brings. | We'll see what nets up.
bc_maybe = Could float. | Maybe so.
bc_me_too = Same lake, same boat. | Same here, shore-friend.
bc_it_does = It does, calm as you like. | That it does.
bc_again = Holed again. | Again, like a slipped net.
bc_lovely = Lovely as a calm.
bc_every_year = Every year, like the ice. | Every year, same as the lake.
bc_wise = Deep thinking. | Wise as the old water.
bc_i_know = I know it like the lake. | Know it to the bottom.
bc_so_they_say = So the shore says. | So it's told on the shore.
bc_again_q = Again? Holed again? | What, again?
bc_of_course = Course. Like water downhill. | Of course, shore-friend.
bc_about_time = About time, and past it. | High time.
bc_mine_too = Mine too. Same lake, same boat. | Same with mine.
bc_ill_try = I'll give it a cast. | I'll try, steady.
bc_nor_me = Nor me, shore-friend. | Nor me. Not a ripple.
bc_they_do = They do, calm as you like. | That they do.
bc_poor_soul = Poor soul, poor soul. | Poor waterlogged soul.
bc_i_wont = I won't. Not a ripple. | Not a word, shore-friend.
bc_every_one = Every one, net and all. | Every last one.
bc_not_me = Not me, shore-friend. | Not this fish.
bc_very_good = Right good, that. | A fair catch, that.
bc_go_on_then = Go on, then. Cast away. | Row on, then.
bc_barely = Barely afloat. | Barely, by a hair.
bc_lucky_you = Full nets for you, then. | Lucky little fish.
bc_every_night = Every night, regular as the lake. | Every blessed night.
bc_nobody = Not a soul on the shore. | Nobody. Empty as a holed net.
bc_for_once = For once, still as the lake. | For once, shore-friend.
bc_i_hope_so = I hope it floats. | Here's hoping, shore-friend.
bc_exactly = Right on the line. | Exactly so.
bc_everything = The whole catch, net besides. | Net, line and all.
bc_true = Deep and true. | True as the lake is deep.
bc_mostly = Mostly, like the lake. | Mostly. Calm, mostly.
bc_gladly = Gladly, shore-friend. | Happy to, calm as you like.
bc_fair_enough = It'll float. | Fair enough, shore-friend.
bc_sorry = Sorry, shore-friend.
bc_every_time = Every time, same as the lake. | Every cast.
bc_very_bad = Right muddied. | Rough water, that.
bc_fair = Fair, shore-friend.
bc_bless_you = The lake bless you. | Bless you, shore-friend.
bc_always_is = Always is, like the lake. | Always is, calm or not.
bc_right = Right you are. | Right, shore-friend.
bc_lucky = A full net. | Lucky, that.
bc_bad_luck = Rough water on you. | Holed luck.
bc_why_q = Why's that, shore-friend? | Why, then?
bc_true_enough = True enough, shore-friend. | It'll float.
bc_they_always_do = Always do, same as the lake. | They always do, shore-friend.
bc_every_day = Every day, calm or rough. | Every blessed day.
bc_very_busy = Run off my paws. | Hauling all day.
bc_im_sorry = I'm sorry, shore-friend. | I'm right sorry.
bc_so_do_i = So do I, shore-friend. | Same lake, same boat.
bc_clever = Well netted. | Clever as a pike.
bc_too_late = Too late. Gone with the current. | Too late, shore-friend.
bc_go_on = Go on, cast it. | Go on, shore-friend.
bc_we_will = We will, steady. | That we will.
bc_they_are = That they are. | They are, deep and true.
bc_so_am_i = So am I, shore-friend. | Same lake, same boat.
bc_let_them = Let them splash. | Let them, calm as you like.
bc_hope_so = Hope it floats. | Here's hoping.
bc_already = Already, shore-friend. | Long since.
bc_i_might = I might, if the water's calm. | Might do.
bc_the_best = The best catch. | Best on the lake.
bc_somebody_has_to = Somebody has to haul it. | Somebody has to, shore-friend.
bc_nothing = Nothing. Not a ripple. | Nothing, shore-friend.
bc_and_q = And? Out with it. | And then, shore-friend?
bc_who_q = Who's that, then? | Who, shore-friend?
bc_it_was = That it was. | It was, calm as you like.
